{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"if [ -f /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed262781723/5/codegen_prompts/3.md ]; then sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed262781723/5/codegen_prompts/3.md; fi\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/5`\n- 题号：`3`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed262781723/5/3.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"3\">\n- `description`：每个彩色对象根据其内部孔洞数量选择对应的\"模板印章\"，然后用该印章替换自身。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格分为左右两个区域，由一条灰色竖线分隔。左侧区域包含若干小型单色模板图案（印章），每个模板用不同颜色绘制，模板之间在垂直方向上用黑色行分隔，从上到下依次排列（第一个模板对应\"0 个孔洞\"，第二个对应\"1 个孔洞\"，第三个对应\"2 个孔洞\"，以此类推）。右侧区域包含若干较大的彩色对象（与模板颜色不同），每个对象内部可能包含 0 个、1 个或多个完全被对象像素包围的黑色孔洞（4-连通封闭区域）。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 数出右侧每个对象内部的孔洞数量 n。\n  - 从左侧模板列表中选取第 n 个模板（从 0 开始计数）。\n  - 将该模板以对象的左上角为锚点绘制到输出网格中，使用对象自身的颜色（而非模板原来的颜色）。\n  - 擦除原始对象，左侧模板区域和灰色分隔线在输出中不保留。\n  - 输出网格仅包含右侧区域的范围。\n- `Key insight`：左侧的模板序列是一个按索引查找的\"字典\"，而索引键是右侧对象内部封闭孔洞的数量——解题者需要先发现孔洞计数与模板顺序之间的映射，再发现输出颜色来自对象而非模板。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/58490d8a, eval/dbff022c, eval/89565ca0, eval/581f7754, train/753ea09b, train/2601afb7, train/42f83767, train/458e3a53\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3595\nmechanism=0.4741\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:sprite, object:template, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/58490d8a.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored sprites s outside the black legend bbox\",\n  \"dst\": \"follow pixels d inside the black legend bbox (one per key color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s reduces to 1 dot placed left-to-right starting at pos(d) with 1 bg pixel gap, replacing d\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3501\nmechanism=0.4229\nlexical=0.0129\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:hole, object:sprite, object:template, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3259\nmechanism=0.4251\nlexical=0.0131\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:axis, object:closed_region, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/89565ca0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"colored partitioned frame line drawings; ignore scattered pad-color pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack one horizontal bar per frame, ordered by count of enclosed background regions then color id\",\n  \"transform\": \"bar length = number of enclosed 4-connected background regions; pad right with the scattered color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height = number of frame colors, width = maximum enclosed-region count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3116\nmechanism=0.3364\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:axis, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/581f7754.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"multi-color sprite s with a unique marker color (minority color inside s)\",\n  \"dst\": \"align to isolated border pixel d (same marker color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.marker.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate s: if d on top/bottom edge → align col(s.marker) to col(d); else → align row(s.marker) to row(d); clip out-of-bounds pixels\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"753ea09b\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2849\nmechanism=0.3140\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/753ea09b.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the small background compartments bounded by the long contour color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and two foreground colors.\n- The more frequent foreground color forms long 8-connected contour fragments.\n- The other foreground color is a fixed distractor/obstacle color that partitions\n  the background but is never recolored.\n\nRule:\n- Split the background into 4-connected regions using all foreground pixels as\n  barriers.\n- Keep the two largest background regions as the outside of the contour drawing.\n- Recolor every other background region that touches a long contour component\n  to the contour color; leave all foreground pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe apparent area fill is exactly a component-level fill of bounded background\ncompartments, not a row-wise interpolation between individual contour pixels.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected background regions partitioned by long 8-connected contour-color components and obstacle-color pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor each non-exterior background region touching a long contour component to the contour color; keep the two largest background regions and all foreground pixels unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2958\nmechanism=0.3802\nlexical=0.0146\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/2601afb7.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2601afb7.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
